    "evidence": "EC-0419 states a claim period of 1-15 October 2026 and lists seven receipts whose dates are as printed (DD/MM/YYYY). Six fall inside the period: R1 02/10/2026 (2 Oct), R2 03/10/2026 (3 Oct), R3 06/10/2026 (6 Oct), R4 09/10/2026 (9 Oct), R5 11/10/2026 (11 Oct), R6 14/10/2026 (14 Oct). R7 (taxi, station to client site, GBP 21.30) is dated 17/10/2026, i.e. 17 October 2026, two days after the period closed. The claim is a universal ('every receipt'), so one exception falsifies it as worded. The DD/MM/YYYY reading is fixed by the record's own note and confirmed by the data: a MM/DD reading would require month 14 for R6 and month 17 for R7, which is impossible. Making the period inclusive of 15 October does not help, since 17 October lies beyond it either way; the submission date of 20 October does not extend the claim period; and no note permits late receipts. The arithmetic is not in issue - the seven receipts sum to 375.14, matching the stated total, so R7's 21.30 is included in the amount claimed. This is a date-scope defect confined to R7. Uncertainty acknowledged: I have the dates as recorded on the claim sheet and not the underlying scans, so if R7's printed date were shown to be a transcription error the claim would hold; nothing on the record suggests that.",
